1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,000
A runaway freighter smashes into a wharf, endangering thousands of lives.

2
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:11,000
Dozens die in a grain elevator explosion caused by dust.

3
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:18,000
The roof of a sports arena falls 60 feet to the ground, sending five construction workers to their deaths.

4
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:22,000
Britain's dream of lighter-than-air travel ends in tragedy.

5
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:27,000
And a bridge failure in New York brings about deadly consequences.

6
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:31,000
Now, Engineering Disasters on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:41,880 --> 00:00:47,040
Along the west bank of the Mississippi River lies the city of West Wego, Louisiana.

8
00:00:47,960 --> 00:00:54,540
On December 22, 1977, the eyes of the nation were fixed on this small southern city

9
00:00:54,541 --> 00:00:58,400
when a 250-foot grain elevator exploded.

10
00:00:59,620 --> 00:01:03,540
It was heard by people over 10 miles away from the facility.

11
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:07,580
The energy released in this explosion was equivalent to many hundreds,

12
00:01:07,840 --> 00:01:09,660
if not thousands of pounds of TNT.

13
00:01:10,220 --> 00:01:14,200
There was fire and smoke coming from the tops of the silos.

14
00:01:14,201 --> 00:01:18,580
There was debris everywhere. It was total devastation.

15
00:01:19,300 --> 00:01:23,580
Perhaps the only thing more shocking than the magnitude of the explosion was the cause.

16
00:01:24,300 --> 00:01:25,000
Grain dust.

17
00:01:25,900 --> 00:01:31,960
Any material that can burn will burn rapidly and explosively if it's small enough.

18
00:01:32,780 --> 00:01:35,000
Grain produces dust when it's handled.

19
00:01:35,440 --> 00:01:38,440
That dust burns so rapidly it's explosive.

20
00:01:38,940 --> 00:01:43,580
The disaster in West Wego was not the first dust explosion at a grain elevator facility.

21
00:01:44,580 --> 00:01:49,700
But its severity was unmatched, and it would serve as a wake-up call for the entire industry.

22
00:01:49,701 --> 00:01:55,640
because of their design green elevators are highly susceptible to dust explosions

23
00:01:55,641 --> 00:02:02,960
the actual elevator at a grain elevator facility typically consists of a multitude of buckets

24
00:02:02,961 --> 00:02:09,020
connected to a vertical conveyor belt when a barge is unloaded the bucket elevator scoops

25
00:02:09,021 --> 00:02:16,280
up grain and carries it to the head house from there it is sorted by grain type and dropped into

26
00:02:16,281 --> 00:02:19,120
concrete silos.

27
00:02:19,121 --> 00:02:23,960
Grain elevators are designed to move tremendous quantities of grain.

28
00:02:23,961 --> 00:02:26,060
When you handle these materials,

29
00:02:26,061 --> 00:02:28,560
the generation of dust is inevitable.

30
00:02:28,561 --> 00:02:31,700
If you look at the four factors required for a dust explosion,

31
00:02:31,701 --> 00:02:34,820
the presence of dust is ever-present.

32
00:02:34,821 --> 00:02:38,640
We have enclosure or confinement of the structure itself

33
00:02:38,641 --> 00:02:40,340
and oxygen in the air.

34
00:02:40,341 --> 00:02:43,740
Therefore, all that's needed is a spark or ignition source

35
00:02:43,741 --> 00:02:44,740
to have a dust explosion.

36
00:02:50,160 --> 00:02:51,600
In the United States,

37
00:02:52,060 --> 00:02:54,160
60% of the grain that is exported

38
00:02:54,161 --> 00:02:56,480
is carried along the Mississippi River,

39
00:02:57,380 --> 00:02:59,320
making Westwego a natural location

40
00:02:59,321 --> 00:03:00,220
for a grain elevator.

41
00:03:01,100 --> 00:03:02,580
And in 1977,

42
00:03:03,240 --> 00:03:04,260
the Westwego facility

43
00:03:04,261 --> 00:03:05,960
was a major employer to the city.

44
00:03:06,520 --> 00:03:08,180
When Mark got the job,

45
00:03:08,240 --> 00:03:09,340
you know, he was happy with it

46
00:03:09,341 --> 00:03:10,620
because it was so close to home.

47
00:03:10,840 --> 00:03:11,680
I never thought it was

48
00:03:11,681 --> 00:03:13,020
a dangerous place to work.

49
00:03:13,740 --> 00:03:18,580
Before Marc Labiche left for work, he alluded to a potential danger at the facility.

50
00:03:19,080 --> 00:03:26,100
They were supposed to close the grain elevator down for cleaning, and I know because Marc had told me this.

51
00:03:26,460 --> 00:03:31,180
On the morning of December 22nd, Marc Labiche was working in the grain elevator's head house.

52
00:03:32,120 --> 00:03:37,620
An unusual cold front was passing through West Wego, dropping the humidity to a very low level.

53
00:03:38,400 --> 00:03:41,100
With static electricity and grain dust in the air,

54
00:03:41,700 --> 00:03:44,460
the elements needed for an explosion came together.

55
00:03:44,820 --> 00:03:48,020
About 9.15, I heard an explosion,

56
00:03:48,440 --> 00:03:51,940
and my daughter and I went up to the river road.

57
00:03:52,380 --> 00:03:57,320
All we could see was a lot of dust and a lot of concrete all over the place.

58
00:03:57,780 --> 00:04:00,420
There were people walking around covered in grain dust.

59
00:04:00,421 --> 00:04:02,900
Many of them were digging through debris,

60
00:04:03,020 --> 00:04:06,280
trying to find co-workers and things like that.

61
00:04:06,281 --> 00:04:10,520
The helicopters were there probably within 30 to 40 minutes after the blast,

62
00:04:10,960 --> 00:04:13,700
and they were trying to rescue people off the tops of the silos.

63
00:04:14,200 --> 00:04:17,580
The radio said it was the river side of the grain elevator,

64
00:04:17,720 --> 00:04:20,400
and I said, thank God, because Mark's working in the headhouse.

65
00:04:21,140 --> 00:04:24,260
But when we got there, it was the headhouse that blew.

66
00:04:25,120 --> 00:04:27,540
Approximately 100 feet of the headhouse was destroyed.

67
00:04:29,160 --> 00:04:32,340
Fractured concrete rained down on the grain facility's control room,

68
00:04:32,820 --> 00:04:33,520
killing dozens.

69
00:04:34,400 --> 00:04:36,420
Among them was Mark Labiche.

70
00:04:37,680 --> 00:04:41,120
The shockwave from the first explosion traveled through the facility,

71
00:04:41,900 --> 00:04:45,480
causing additional grain dust to become airborne and to combust.

72
00:04:46,180 --> 00:04:49,340
A number of secondary explosions ignited instantaneously,

73
00:04:50,280 --> 00:04:51,780
completely destroying the facility.

74
00:04:52,120 --> 00:04:57,080
The explosion in West Wego involved a very large quantity of dust

75
00:04:57,081 --> 00:04:59,420
built up in a very large structure.

76
00:04:59,421 --> 00:05:04,420
Due to the magnitude of this explosion, the exact cause cannot be pinpointed,

77
00:05:05,200 --> 00:05:09,660
but is likely a malfunctioning mechanical component on the bucket elevator,

78
00:05:09,820 --> 00:05:13,860
such as a failed bearing, or on a ventilation fan in the headhouse.

79
00:05:14,180 --> 00:05:17,880
The resulting mechanical friction would have produced a fatal spark,

80
00:05:18,340 --> 00:05:20,500
or enough heat to cause the dust to ignite.

81
00:05:21,480 --> 00:05:23,840
36 people died in the accident.

82
00:05:24,480 --> 00:05:27,100
It remains the deadliest graindust explosion on record.

83
00:05:29,420 --> 00:05:38,360
In the years that followed the West Wego disaster, the grain elevator industry has taken extensive measures to minimize the potential for grain dust explosions.

84
00:05:39,340 --> 00:05:48,940
Today, roughly 20 miles outside of West Wego, in Destrehan, Louisiana, the Bungie Grain Elevator utilizes a variety of safety methods found throughout the industry.

85
00:05:49,880 --> 00:05:56,780
The incidence of 1977 in the grain industry was kind of a wake-up call, if you will,

86
00:05:56,840 --> 00:06:00,980
for the whole industry to realize that we had to give a renewed focus

87
00:06:00,981 --> 00:06:08,020
and much more emphasis on root cause analysis of what causes these type of explosions.

88
00:06:09,060 --> 00:06:11,860
One of the critical safety measures implemented by the industry

89
00:06:11,861 --> 00:06:14,660
is to closely monitor potential ignition points,

90
00:06:15,640 --> 00:06:17,220
primarily the bucket elevator,

91
00:06:17,720 --> 00:06:20,820
which is the cause of 40% of all grain dust explosions.

92
00:06:22,080 --> 00:06:27,000
If a bearing overheats or the conveyor belt is misaligned,

93
00:06:28,140 --> 00:06:30,560
heat sensors located throughout the elevator legs

94
00:06:30,561 --> 00:06:33,300
will notify personnel of a potential explosion.

95
00:06:33,301 --> 00:06:37,760
We have over 700 of these discrete points

96
00:06:37,761 --> 00:06:40,060
that we're actually monitoring the temperature all the time,

97
00:06:40,280 --> 00:06:43,560
and that information is conveyed back to the control room

98
00:06:43,561 --> 00:06:47,540
that is sensing these temperatures, giving us alarm if it gets to a certain level

99
00:06:47,541 --> 00:06:51,440
and can even shut down the equipment automatically if it gets to a higher level.

100
00:06:52,400 --> 00:06:54,700
However, if there is an accidental explosion,

101
00:06:55,480 --> 00:06:57,760
measures have been taken to contain the blast wave,

102
00:06:58,320 --> 00:07:01,080
thus preventing the often deadlier secondary explosions.

103
00:07:02,160 --> 00:07:05,340
Five, four, three, two, one.

104
00:07:06,980 --> 00:07:12,380
How it works is that the suppression device actually senses the increase in pressure

105
00:07:12,381 --> 00:07:15,920
caused by the early stages of an explosion

106
00:07:15,921 --> 00:07:20,740
and would dump a chemical into that bucket elevator casing

107
00:07:20,741 --> 00:07:24,300
that would immediately suppress and eliminate the explosion.

108
00:07:25,140 --> 00:07:28,560
The chemical agent is dispersed within 80 milliseconds,

109
00:07:29,640 --> 00:07:31,540
stopping the explosion in its tracks.

110
00:07:32,940 --> 00:07:36,140
Making sure that dust doesn't accumulate around the facility

111
00:07:36,141 --> 00:07:39,060
is also vital to minimizing secondary explosions.

112
00:07:39,061 --> 00:07:47,440
And as a direct result of the incident in West Wego, grain elevator headhouses are no longer located above employee offices.

113
00:07:48,140 --> 00:07:53,120
Spreading the facilities out, moving the control rooms to remote locations, all of those came at a cost.

114
00:07:53,600 --> 00:07:57,300
Efficiency, but it was worth it from the safety payback.

115
00:07:57,640 --> 00:07:58,840
It's a real success story.

116
00:07:59,360 --> 00:08:06,400
We went from a time, in a peak year, over 60 people were killed in agricultural dust explosions in the late 70s.

117
00:08:06,401 --> 00:08:12,680
and a great reduction in risk has been achieved through the engineering evaluation of these types of accidents

118
00:08:12,681 --> 00:08:15,560
and taking appropriate steps based on the knowledge learned.

119
00:08:16,220 --> 00:08:17,320
And the numbers prove it.

120
00:08:17,980 --> 00:08:24,780
From 1976 to 1985, there were 143 deaths as a result of grain dust explosions.

121
00:08:26,280 --> 00:08:31,680
From 1986 to 2003, that number dropped to 33.

122
00:08:31,681 --> 00:08:37,640
Five days after the grain elevator explosion in West Wego, Louisiana,

123
00:08:38,280 --> 00:08:41,200
a grain elevator in Galveston, Texas also exploded,

124
00:08:41,840 --> 00:08:43,820
taking the lives of 18 people.

125
00:08:44,820 --> 00:08:47,560
Engineering disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

126
00:08:50,340 --> 00:08:53,100
On the morning of April 5, 1987,

127
00:08:53,101 --> 00:08:58,360
a light shower fell on the already rain-soaked I-90 thruway bridge

128
00:08:58,361 --> 00:09:01,480
crossing over Schoharie Creek in upstate New York.

129
00:09:01,680 --> 00:09:08,400
It was the final vestige of a torrential downpour that caused the Schoharie Creek to reach a near-record flood level.

130
00:09:09,660 --> 00:09:15,720
As traffic crossed the overpass, without warning, a 200-foot span of the bridge collapsed.

131
00:09:16,240 --> 00:09:19,820
Two vehicles immediately plummeted 80 feet into the raging creek below.

132
00:09:20,960 --> 00:09:24,960
Before the bridge could be closed to traffic, three more cars fell through the void.

133
00:09:25,980 --> 00:09:27,240
There were no survivors.

134
00:09:27,241 --> 00:09:33,900
The tragic collapse of the Schoharie Creek Bridge led to an immediate investigation to

135
00:09:33,901 --> 00:09:38,500
determine the cause of the failure.

136
00:09:38,501 --> 00:09:44,640
The 540-foot-long bridge over Schoharie Creek was part of the New York State Thruway, a

137
00:09:44,641 --> 00:09:51,860
superhighway connecting New York City to Albany and then west to Buffalo.

138
00:09:51,861 --> 00:09:56,760
Construction of the original bridge began in 1953, featuring a design that was standard

139
00:09:56,761 --> 00:10:02,978
for its time. It had two abutments, four piers, and five spans.

140
00:10:02,979 --> 00:10:05,840
The span lengths on both ends

141
00:10:05,841 --> 00:10:09,358
coming off the abutments were 100-foot spans.

142
00:10:09,359 --> 00:10:12,720
Spans 2 and 4 were 110-foot spans, and span

143
00:10:12,721 --> 00:10:14,088
3 was 120-foot span.

144
00:10:14,089 --> 00:10:19,080
The four piers that supported the superstructure consisted of two columns

145
00:10:19,081 --> 00:10:23,608
connected to a subbase, or plinth, resting on spread footing.

146
00:10:23,609 --> 00:10:25,760
To protect the bridge against

147
00:10:25,761 --> 00:10:30,660
the potential erosion of the riverbed, the contractor attempted to use steel piles to

148
00:10:30,661 --> 00:10:31,480
anchor the footings.

149
00:10:32,060 --> 00:10:37,760
When time came for the contractor to drive those piles, he found out that the material

150
00:10:37,761 --> 00:10:44,260
was glacial tilt, which is a very compact material of stones, rocks, and earth that

151
00:10:44,261 --> 00:10:48,860
was compacted by hundreds of, if not thousands of feet of glaciation on top.

152
00:10:48,861 --> 00:10:53,760
There was a number of boulders, boulders of different sizes, making it very difficult

153
00:10:53,761 --> 00:10:57,080
to drive piling into the bottom of the river.

154
00:10:57,440 --> 00:11:02,140
The designers finally concluded that the glacial till at this site

155
00:11:02,141 --> 00:11:06,400
was sturdy enough to support the foundation system of the substructures.

156
00:11:07,160 --> 00:11:11,540
The 1951 flood of the Schoharie Creek also played a role in the decision.

157
00:11:12,080 --> 00:11:13,600
It was the largest flood on record

158
00:11:13,601 --> 00:11:16,720
and was the result of a severe storm that struck upstate New York.

159
00:11:16,880 --> 00:11:22,260
The resulting precipitation produced a peak flow of 37,900 cubic feet per second.

160
00:11:22,920 --> 00:11:25,320
But because that flood was believed to be a rare occurrence,

161
00:11:25,580 --> 00:11:28,240
the bearing capacity of glacial till was felt to be sufficient.

162
00:11:28,680 --> 00:11:32,320
Therefore, piles were considered unnecessary and riprap was used instead.

163
00:11:33,280 --> 00:11:38,140
Riprap is large angular rock that is placed around the foundation

164
00:11:38,141 --> 00:11:42,840
to protect the footing and the substructure from water flow.

165
00:11:43,180 --> 00:11:45,340
This is not foolproof.

166
00:11:45,341 --> 00:11:50,560
In other words, it works some of the time and it needs continuous checking

167
00:11:50,561 --> 00:11:55,520
and investigating that the riprap stays in the original position.

168
00:11:56,180 --> 00:12:00,620
But this crucial element of safety was hindered by the bridge's ambiguous design plans,

169
00:12:01,260 --> 00:12:04,280
which did not specify exactly how the rocks should be placed.

170
00:12:05,040 --> 00:12:09,740
If you looked at the south elevation, it showed riprap going down around the footing.

171
00:12:09,741 --> 00:12:15,880
They also showed steel sheeting in around the foundation that eventually would be filled with riprap.

172
00:12:16,060 --> 00:12:19,260
If you take a look at the cross section at the center line of the bridge,

173
00:12:19,261 --> 00:12:23,300
It just showed a covering of riprap over the glacial till,

174
00:12:23,460 --> 00:12:28,360
which made it somewhat difficult for the contractor to decide which way it was really supposed to be built.

175
00:12:29,100 --> 00:12:33,360
Investigators concluded that riprap was placed only around the surface of the piers.

176
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:38,960
The Skoharie Creek Bridge opened to traffic in October of 1954.

177
00:12:40,520 --> 00:12:44,600
Almost one year later, the integrity of the structure would be severely tested.

178
00:12:44,601 --> 00:12:52,720
In August of 1955, Hurricane Diane dropped nine inches of rain along the northeastern United States,

179
00:12:53,820 --> 00:12:59,320
increasing the volume of the Schoharie Creek to more than twice the flow brought by the 1951 flood.

180
00:13:01,360 --> 00:13:03,700
From the surface, the bridge appeared intact,

181
00:13:04,380 --> 00:13:08,620
but the high flow had attacked the riprap located on the upstream side of the piers,

182
00:13:09,440 --> 00:13:13,180
pushing the rocks downstream and making the footings vulnerable to scour.

183
00:13:13,660 --> 00:13:20,480
Scour is the creation of a hole, usually at the face of a pier or an abutment.

184
00:13:21,180 --> 00:13:25,520
What happens is that the water hits the face of a pier.

185
00:13:26,260 --> 00:13:29,580
It usually goes down and then up again.

186
00:13:29,581 --> 00:13:33,620
And what it does, it takes the earth, then cobbles and stones,

187
00:13:34,120 --> 00:13:38,200
and creates a hole right in the face of the pier or an abutment.

188
00:13:38,201 --> 00:13:44,180
Well, scour is kind of an interesting animal because of the fact that once scour occurs,

189
00:13:44,540 --> 00:13:48,480
when you have these high floodwaters, as the flood recedes,

190
00:13:48,880 --> 00:13:52,180
these scour holes can actually refill up with material.

191
00:13:52,480 --> 00:13:54,420
So it makes them somewhat hard to detect.

192
00:13:55,640 --> 00:13:58,060
After the record flood of 1955,

193
00:13:58,980 --> 00:14:03,300
four additional floods also exceeded the velocity of the 1951 flood.

194
00:14:03,700 --> 00:14:07,120
Yet the riprap at the Schoharie Creek Bridge was never replaced,

195
00:14:07,121 --> 00:14:10,460
and an underwater inspection of the bridge was never performed.

196
00:14:13,800 --> 00:14:18,480
In April of 1987, a major storm struck upstate New York,

197
00:14:19,100 --> 00:14:20,960
setting the stage for a disaster.

198
00:14:21,760 --> 00:14:26,200
The storm in 1987 was one of the biggest storms on record

199
00:14:26,201 --> 00:14:29,900
and it created a huge amount of flow.

200
00:14:30,400 --> 00:14:32,160
It was something like 12 foot high,

201
00:14:32,640 --> 00:14:34,440
it was going about 10 miles an hour

202
00:14:34,441 --> 00:14:37,300
and it was carrying all kinds of debris.

203
00:14:38,640 --> 00:14:42,540
At 8.45 a.m., the Burtonsville Gauge,

204
00:14:42,760 --> 00:14:44,960
located 13 miles upstream of the bridge,

205
00:14:45,500 --> 00:14:49,520
recorded a peak flow of 64,900 cubic feet per second,

206
00:14:50,120 --> 00:14:51,920
the second highest on record.

207
00:14:52,860 --> 00:14:56,740
Two hours later, the turbulent flow reached the Schoharie Creek Bridge.

208
00:14:57,500 --> 00:15:00,880
A bend in the channel brought the fastest-moving section of the flooded creek

209
00:15:00,881 --> 00:15:03,940
to the glacial till on the upstream side of Pier 3.

210
00:15:04,440 --> 00:15:06,540
now unprotected by riprap.

211
00:15:07,420 --> 00:15:11,420
The soil pressure at the very back end of the scour hole increased significantly,

212
00:15:11,660 --> 00:15:14,540
changing the stress flow throughout the plinth

213
00:15:14,541 --> 00:15:16,780
because you don't have the support at the front end.

214
00:15:17,500 --> 00:15:20,140
And as that scour hole changed,

215
00:15:20,220 --> 00:15:22,960
it changed the stress configuration in the foundation

216
00:15:22,961 --> 00:15:26,020
and finally caused the plinth to fracture.

217
00:15:26,620 --> 00:15:31,440
The plinth fracture at Pier 3 dropped the column 4 feet into a scour hole.

218
00:15:32,420 --> 00:15:37,400
The resulting loss of stability to the superstructure brought two spans of the roadbed,

219
00:15:37,760 --> 00:15:39,700
crashing down into the raging creek.

220
00:15:40,220 --> 00:15:46,920
The debris from the collapse diverted the flow to Pier 2, which failed 90 minutes later, also due to scour.

221
00:15:49,540 --> 00:15:57,280
The reason that the scour hole occurred was that we did not have adequate riprap protection around the scour hole.

222
00:15:57,281 --> 00:16:02,800
This substructure counted on the placement or rip-rap to provide protection for the foundation.

223
00:16:03,140 --> 00:16:07,020
That had to be checked routinely throughout the life of the bridge.

224
00:16:07,640 --> 00:16:13,760
The failure of the Schoharie Creek Bridge brought national attention to the vulnerability of America's bridges to scour.

225
00:16:14,860 --> 00:16:20,660
It spurred new legislation demanding the underwater inspection of bridges on a biannual basis.

226
00:16:20,661 --> 00:16:27,980
The impact of the failure of this bridge has been far and wide throughout the United States and the world.

227
00:16:28,540 --> 00:16:31,540
It forced us to go to the U.S.

228
00:16:31,541 --> 00:16:38,340
Geological Survey and fund a scour study that lasted about 10 years.

229
00:16:39,680 --> 00:16:46,580
Soon after the collapse, the Schoharie Creek Bridge was rebuilt with the intent of making it one of the safest bridges in the country.

230
00:16:47,420 --> 00:16:52,760
It cost probably two and a half times what a normal bridge would have cost.

231
00:16:53,340 --> 00:17:00,700
They drove piles, huge piles, the biggest piles there is, to rock 40 or 50 foot deep.

232
00:17:01,100 --> 00:17:04,400
And this bridge should not collapse again.

233
00:17:04,401 --> 00:17:08,200
It is probably the best built bridge in New York State.

234
00:17:09,380 --> 00:17:13,320
