460
00:34:10,980 --> 00:34:14,880
After the R-101 took off from Cardington, it circled over London,

461
00:34:15,400 --> 00:34:19,000
giving onlookers a chance to gaze at Britain's newest technological marvel.

462
00:34:20,420 --> 00:34:23,120
It then headed for the English Channel on its way to France.

463
00:34:23,820 --> 00:34:26,540
It would be the last time Britain would see the R101.

464
00:34:27,360 --> 00:34:29,800
They took off on a rainy, misty day,

465
00:34:30,200 --> 00:34:35,780
and the winds were along the path of travel in the 30, 35-mile-an-hour range.

466
00:34:36,340 --> 00:34:41,320
Not the most favorable conditions for dirigible travel,

467
00:34:41,321 --> 00:34:47,820
but certainly not the sort of things you would expect to wipe out this particular craft.

468
00:34:48,680 --> 00:34:53,660
But just after 2 a.m., as the R-101 was passing through a rainstorm,

469
00:34:54,280 --> 00:34:56,040
the ship began losing altitude.

470
00:34:56,420 --> 00:35:00,460
The captain assumed that the fabric had become waterlogged and too heavy.

471
00:35:00,860 --> 00:35:05,080
What he didn't know was that the top fabric from the nose back had ripped off,

472
00:35:05,640 --> 00:35:09,400
and the gas bags were exposed to the storm, to the rain, to the wind.

473
00:35:09,401 --> 00:35:13,260
and while it was only a moderate wind, 25 miles an hour,

474
00:35:13,380 --> 00:35:15,760
the airship was going at about 50 miles an hour,

475
00:35:15,840 --> 00:35:19,340
so this was hurricane-force winds blowing on these gas bags.

476
00:35:19,580 --> 00:35:23,480
The high winds violently shook the gas bags closest to the ship's bow,

477
00:35:24,000 --> 00:35:26,180
causing them to chafe against the airship's frame.

478
00:35:26,640 --> 00:35:30,760
The added stresses turned the numerous pinhole leaks into a fatal fissure,

479
00:35:31,260 --> 00:35:35,300
accelerating the loss of hydrogen and causing the dirigible to nose over,

480
00:35:36,040 --> 00:35:37,720
realizing that a crash was inevitable.

481
00:35:38,720 --> 00:35:41,240
The helmsman cut the ship's engines to soften the landing.

482
00:35:41,740 --> 00:35:44,620
It's estimated they hit the ground at less than 13 miles an hour.

483
00:35:44,940 --> 00:35:47,440
Not even a bad bicycle fall, if the truth be known.

484
00:35:47,760 --> 00:35:52,900
However, a spark from the twisted metal ignited the gas bag closest to the bow of the ship,

485
00:35:53,620 --> 00:35:59,140
setting off a chain reaction of fast-moving fires that spread from the bow to the stern,

486
00:36:00,020 --> 00:36:01,560
engulfing the airship in flames.

487
00:36:02,940 --> 00:36:06,640
Of the 54 people on board, all but six died.

488
00:36:07,720 --> 00:36:09,140
including Lord Thompson.

489
00:36:11,440 --> 00:36:16,440
Several of the survivors testified that the front end of the airship's outer covering had ripped off,

490
00:36:17,280 --> 00:36:20,480
a crucial piece of evidence that may have explained the loss of altitude.

491
00:36:24,040 --> 00:36:26,920
Covered by the dark cloud of the R-101's failure,

492
00:36:27,760 --> 00:36:31,640
the sister ship R-100 was dismantled and sold for scrap.

493
00:36:31,641 --> 00:36:39,020
The skeletal frame of the R101, which represented the best technology in British lighter-than-air

494
00:36:39,021 --> 00:36:46,540
travel, was eventually sold for scrap as well, putting an end to the British airship program.

495
00:36:46,541 --> 00:36:51,800
The German Zeppelin company purchased 5,000 kilograms of derallument, an aluminum-based

496
00:36:51,801 --> 00:36:54,960
alloy from the crashed R101.

497
00:36:54,961 --> 00:36:58,420
Some believe the material was used on the Hindenburg.

498
00:36:58,421 --> 00:37:01,520
Engineering Disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

499
00:37:03,660 --> 00:37:06,780
They are modern-day versions of the Roman Colosseum,

500
00:37:07,320 --> 00:37:10,100
built to entertain tens of thousands of people.

501
00:37:10,920 --> 00:37:15,560
But if engineering mistakes are made, they can collapse without a moment's notice.

502
00:37:16,200 --> 00:37:20,400
And in the late 1970s, arena collapses became eerily common.

503
00:37:21,940 --> 00:37:26,380
In 1978, the Hartford Civic Center suffered a structural failure

504
00:37:26,381 --> 00:37:28,560
just hours after hosting a sporting event.

505
00:37:29,460 --> 00:37:33,680
In 1979, the Kemper Sports Arena also came crashing down.

506
00:37:34,600 --> 00:37:37,580
Both arenas had a common point of failure, the roof.

507
00:37:38,220 --> 00:37:42,100
What normally causes roof failures really has two origins.

508
00:37:42,420 --> 00:37:44,900
When structures that have been completed fail,

509
00:37:45,420 --> 00:37:47,340
they fail either because they're overloaded

510
00:37:47,341 --> 00:37:51,140
or because there's some problem of deterioration perhaps

511
00:37:51,141 --> 00:37:57,960
or some other issue where the structure's performance under load is different than the assumptions.

512
00:37:59,220 --> 00:38:03,060
The roofs that never make it out of construction, that never are completed,

513
00:38:03,660 --> 00:38:05,920
those roofs collapse during construction,

514
00:38:06,400 --> 00:38:10,400
and one of the most prevalent causes are some form of instability.

515
00:38:12,160 --> 00:38:16,440
Such was the case in 1979 with the Rosemont Horizon.

516
00:38:16,441 --> 00:38:28,360
In 1978, the village of Rosemont, Illinois, a Chicago suburb, broke ground on an ambitious 18,000-seat capacity sports complex.

517
00:38:31,720 --> 00:38:37,320
When we had the initial press conference and announced that we were going to build an arena, they said,

518
00:38:37,840 --> 00:38:41,020
Now, wait a minute, Mayor. How many people live in Rosemont?

519
00:38:41,021 --> 00:38:43,520
and I said 4,000

520
00:38:43,521 --> 00:38:46,540
and you're going to build a convention center

521
00:38:46,541 --> 00:38:49,040
an arena to seat how many?

522
00:38:49,640 --> 00:38:50,940
I said about 18,000

523
00:38:50,941 --> 00:38:52,820
he said well

524
00:38:52,821 --> 00:38:55,520
how are you going to fill the arena?

525
00:38:57,040 --> 00:38:58,200
I said well you know really

526
00:38:58,201 --> 00:39:00,920
the arena is going to serve more than just Rosemont

527
00:39:00,921 --> 00:39:03,720
we're in a metropolitan area here

528
00:39:03,721 --> 00:39:06,280
that has got 8 million people living in it

529
00:39:06,281 --> 00:39:08,740
so if you have a ticket

530
00:39:08,741 --> 00:39:09,920
even if you're not in Rosemont

531
00:39:09,921 --> 00:39:10,621
we'll let you in

532
00:39:11,020 --> 00:39:17,480
The decision to build the arena was largely influenced by Rosemont's close proximity to Chicago's O'Hare Airport,

533
00:39:18,360 --> 00:39:19,940
one of the busiest in the world.

534
00:39:20,820 --> 00:39:24,420
But the airport was responsible for a great deal of noise pollution,

535
00:39:25,240 --> 00:39:27,600
enough to demand a unique type of roof for the arena.

536
00:39:28,360 --> 00:39:32,940
Wood was used for a dampening effect from the aircraft noise.

537
00:39:33,940 --> 00:39:38,980
It is practically dead center of one of the glide paths coming in on the runways.

538
00:39:38,981 --> 00:39:42,000
So the wood was used for sound insulation.

539
00:39:42,780 --> 00:39:45,800
The only thing that was better would have been cork.

540
00:39:45,980 --> 00:39:48,880
And of course, cork, you can't make cork stand up there.

541
00:39:49,320 --> 00:39:54,400
The design for the roof of the Rosemont Horizon called for 16 glue laminated arches,

542
00:39:55,000 --> 00:39:57,240
each spanning 288 feet.

543
00:39:58,020 --> 00:40:02,280
If you can keep the arch in a single plane, like a piece of paper,

544
00:40:02,560 --> 00:40:07,260
it is a very efficient structural system, one that's very strong for its weight.

545
00:40:07,261 --> 00:40:12,680
The thing that's important to recognize, though, is you must make efforts to keep it in plane.

546
00:40:13,180 --> 00:40:18,480
As soon as the arch begins to move out of plane, in other words, to twist or to rotate,

547
00:40:18,800 --> 00:40:20,900
it immediately begins to lose strength.

548
00:40:21,080 --> 00:40:26,980
It creates conditions which allows the arch to behave badly, and ultimately it will collapse.

549
00:40:27,860 --> 00:40:31,160
Each arch of the roof was fashioned from three distinct pieces.

550
00:40:31,880 --> 00:40:34,760
Two end trusses were connected to two concrete buttresses,

551
00:40:34,761 --> 00:40:37,380
and then the crown truss was placed in the middle.

552
00:40:38,680 --> 00:40:41,400
The two end pieces were then spliced to the centerpiece

553
00:40:41,401 --> 00:40:43,680
by prefabricated metal sheeting.

554
00:40:44,640 --> 00:40:46,500
Running perpendicular between the arches

555
00:40:46,501 --> 00:40:49,660
was a series of wood beams known as purlins.

556
00:40:50,680 --> 00:40:53,040
Those purlins serve two functions.

557
00:40:53,280 --> 00:40:55,820
They support the roof decking and the roof materials,

558
00:40:55,821 --> 00:40:59,900
but more importantly, they laterally support the arch,

559
00:41:00,020 --> 00:41:01,980
which is a very slender element.

560
00:41:01,981 --> 00:41:06,980
The arches are absolutely critically supported by the purlins.

561
00:41:07,360 --> 00:41:11,000
As the arches were being assembled, problems quickly arose.

562
00:41:11,980 --> 00:41:14,280
The arch sections didn't line up with the splice holes.

563
00:41:15,420 --> 00:41:19,920
Also, the spacing between the arches was off by as much as 12 inches in one span,

564
00:41:20,180 --> 00:41:22,700
making the purlins difficult to place.

565
00:41:24,100 --> 00:41:27,980
Because of the misalignment, some of the connection holes were enlarged,

566
00:41:28,680 --> 00:41:30,380
and many of the bolts were omitted,

567
00:41:30,840 --> 00:41:33,660
Temporary bolts called drift pins were used instead.

568
00:41:34,200 --> 00:41:37,780
One of the principal differences between a wood structure and a steel structure

569
00:41:37,781 --> 00:41:41,540
relates to the connections and the bolting processes.

570
00:41:42,220 --> 00:41:47,880
The erectors in the Chicago area would rely on steel erectors,

571
00:41:48,120 --> 00:41:51,440
people that are familiar with such structures as the Sears Tower,

572
00:41:52,040 --> 00:41:54,920
John Hancock, and other notable steel structures.

573
00:41:54,921 --> 00:41:59,220
In those structures, it's common to not employ all the bolts,

574
00:41:59,221 --> 00:42:00,900
to put all the bolts in the connection

575
00:42:00,901 --> 00:42:05,420
because of the difficulty in fitting up a multi-bolted connection.

576
00:42:05,780 --> 00:42:09,160
In wood, we have to be careful that all of those bolts

577
00:42:09,161 --> 00:42:13,280
may be necessary for our delicate friend, the arch,

578
00:42:13,281 --> 00:42:15,800
who has a tendency to get out of plane.

579
00:42:16,680 --> 00:42:19,760
In the early morning hours of August 13, 1979,

580
00:42:20,540 --> 00:42:22,680
the final arch of the roof was being erected

581
00:42:22,681 --> 00:42:26,580
when a 20-mile-per-hour gust of wind blowing perpendicular to the arches

582
00:42:26,581 --> 00:42:28,040
led to disaster.

583
00:42:28,041 --> 00:42:39,571
The arches became unstable and collapsed moving toward the west, and it formed somewhat of a progressive type collapse in which one arch failed, released the lateral

584
00:42:39,572 --> 00:42:46,700
bracing to the next arch, loaded it laterally, it failed, then the next, then the next, then the next.

585
00:42:46,920 --> 00:42:52,060
The timber roof fell 60 feet, splintering on the ground as if it were a pile of matchsticks.

586
00:42:53,040 --> 00:42:55,640
Five construction workers were killed in the accident.

587
00:42:55,641 --> 00:42:58,880
The arena was completely destroyed.

588
00:43:00,340 --> 00:43:03,260
An investigation into the failure of the Rosemont Horizon

589
00:43:03,261 --> 00:43:09,260
found that 54% of the 966 required bolts were missing from the roof.

590
00:43:10,320 --> 00:43:16,660
Of the 444 bolts that were in place, 338 had no nuts.

591
00:43:17,280 --> 00:43:21,220
The principal reasons for the collapse of the roof

592
00:43:21,221 --> 00:43:26,480
relate to the absence of lateral bracing on the main arch members.

593
00:43:27,060 --> 00:43:32,880
That lateral bracing is accomplished by fully effective connections

594
00:43:32,881 --> 00:43:34,600
between the purlins and the brace

595
00:43:34,601 --> 00:43:37,260
to make sure that the arch can't move laterally.

596
00:43:38,540 --> 00:43:40,260
In the days that followed,

597
00:43:40,920 --> 00:43:43,540
the decision was made to rebuild the Rosemont Horizon

598
00:43:43,541 --> 00:43:45,900
following exactly the same design plans,

599
00:43:46,760 --> 00:43:48,460
but with a different contractor.

600
00:43:55,749 --> 00:44:06,035
When we erected the roof the second time, each 24-foot section was completely installed and 100% complete before we moved on to the next section, which in essence

601
00:44:06,036 --> 00:44:10,860
allowed each one to be 100% secure before we moved the supporting structure.

602
00:44:10,861 --> 00:44:16,740
The Rosemont Horizon has been renamed the Allstate Arena and has served its community

603
00:44:16,741 --> 00:44:19,240
for more than 20 years.

604
00:44:19,241 --> 00:44:23,660
Because the new contractor followed the same design plans, the roof failure of the arena

605
00:44:23,661 --> 00:44:28,640
demonstrates that a structure's stability relies as much on the diligence of the builder

606
00:44:28,641 --> 00:44:30,680
as it does on the skill of the architect.

